[SPEAKER_01]: You don't question it.

400
00:24:35,116 --> 00:24:38,099
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, there are prospects to then, like, get away from it.

401
00:24:38,479 --> 00:24:40,381
[SPEAKER_01]: And you will then automatically delight in it.

402
00:24:40,681 --> 00:24:41,482
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's what Buddha said.

403
00:24:41,662 --> 00:24:42,903
[SPEAKER_01]: Gratification is the delight.

404
00:24:45,300 --> 00:24:47,161
[SPEAKER_01]: It's your delight in the sense object.

405
00:24:47,201 --> 00:24:48,822
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the gratification of sensuality.

406
00:24:48,882 --> 00:24:50,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Not engagement with the sense objects.

407
00:24:50,463 --> 00:24:52,204
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you can get everything you want.

408
00:24:52,744 --> 00:24:54,165
[SPEAKER_01]: It's you delighting.

409
00:24:54,785 --> 00:24:57,887
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already when you start satisfying your desires.

410
00:24:58,587 --> 00:25:02,169
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you accept the possibility of satisfaction.

411
00:25:02,309 --> 00:25:07,392
[SPEAKER_01]: As in, satisfaction over there, means escaping this presently enduring pain over here.

412
00:25:10,181 --> 00:25:11,563
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, sure, sure.

413
00:25:12,264 --> 00:25:13,606
[SPEAKER_01]: It's on that level, certainly.

414
00:25:13,626 --> 00:25:18,432
[SPEAKER_01]: You might not be explicitly thinking, like, clarifying, oh, it's a hope, but yeah, it's on that level.

415
00:25:18,452 --> 00:25:22,758
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the possibility of satisfaction... ...is already delighted in.

416
00:25:22,778 --> 00:25:28,085
[SPEAKER_01]: That's already... that delight in the possibility of satisfaction is where gratification already is.

417
00:25:30,634 --> 00:25:33,617
[SPEAKER_03]: Well, then the danger is mixed in, right?

418
00:25:33,677 --> 00:25:34,498
[SPEAKER_03]: Exactly, yeah.

419
00:25:34,578 --> 00:25:39,542
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you go down the domain that has nothing to do with why you are going down that domain.

420
00:25:40,023 --> 00:25:43,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, again, there's a whole irony contained in any assumption.

421
00:25:45,087 --> 00:25:50,752
[SPEAKER_01]: This unpleasantly arisen experience as a whole, you don't want that pain.

422
00:25:51,933 --> 00:25:55,877
[SPEAKER_01]: So, by not wanting that pain, you go to that which is painful.

423
00:25:56,632 --> 00:26:02,237
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that unpleasant... a pleasant... there is an experience, the whole is the experience of sensuality, as we described it.

424
00:26:03,298 --> 00:26:06,961
[SPEAKER_01]: Whenever there is a sense desire, it's first felt unpleasantly.

425
00:26:07,742 --> 00:26:12,366
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't want that unpleasure, you engage with it.

426
00:26:13,467 --> 00:26:15,068
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, how do you think it's going to be felt?

427
00:26:15,789 --> 00:26:16,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Unpleasantly.

428
00:26:17,030 --> 00:26:19,372
[SPEAKER_01]: So, literally, like scratching the itch, as it was said.

429
00:26:19,392 --> 00:26:20,893
[SPEAKER_01]: Each time you engage, you make it worse.

430
00:26:21,614 --> 00:26:23,876
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, if you clarify this, you realize that

431
00:26:24,943 --> 00:26:28,686
[SPEAKER_01]: Sensuality arises, and it's felt unpleasantly, and you don't want pain.

432
00:26:28,746 --> 00:26:29,667
[SPEAKER_01]: Nobody wants pain.

433
00:26:30,027 --> 00:26:36,833
[SPEAKER_01]: And then you realize sensory strength is then the pleasure in regard to that painful sensuality.

434
00:26:38,434 --> 00:26:43,598
[SPEAKER_01]: Abstaining from engagement with sensuality is how you're going to free yourself from that pain of that sensuality.

435
00:26:43,638 --> 00:26:47,141
[SPEAKER_01]: And you want to be free from it, which is why you keep engaging with it.

436
00:26:47,722 --> 00:26:47,922
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

437
00:26:48,798 --> 00:26:50,699
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sensuality pressures you.

438
00:26:51,199 --> 00:26:54,261
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's underlined by the pain, simultaneously there.

439
00:26:54,961 --> 00:26:59,804
[SPEAKER_01]: And if you have any doubt about it, say no to yourself in regard to desire, and you'll see the pressure that builds up.

440
00:27:00,364 --> 00:27:01,024
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

441
00:27:02,185 --> 00:27:03,345
[SPEAKER_01]: Why are you pressured?

442
00:27:04,186 --> 00:27:06,507
[SPEAKER_01]: Where is the... How is that pressure generated?

443
00:27:07,027 --> 00:27:08,408
[SPEAKER_01]: Pressure towards sense object.

444
00:27:09,406 --> 00:27:18,556
[SPEAKER_01]: It's basically, directly corresponds, simultaneously, to the extent of craving that you maintain in regard to the pain that's being felt.

445
00:27:18,636 --> 00:27:22,761
[SPEAKER_01]: You remove the craving in regard to the pain, there is no pressure towards the sense objects.

446
00:27:23,822 --> 00:27:29,048
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the prospect of the pleasure of sense objects becomes non-applicable.

447
00:27:31,759 --> 00:27:46,449
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why it's important, like, I keep going back to this, I don't want to... like, we can talk about it later, about the danger and so on, escape, but it's all contained in first, then you can discern more within the first principle, and that's that any degree of pleasure...

448
00:27:46,510 --> 00:28:01,040
[SPEAKER_01]: So, imagine, actually, physical engagement with sensual pleasure, amidst of physical engagement with sensual pleasure, the pleasurability, pleasurability of physical engagement,

449
00:28:02,656 --> 00:28:08,179
[SPEAKER_01]: ...is actually the delighting in freedom from simultaneously enduring pain.

450
00:28:09,879 --> 00:28:11,660
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not coming from the senses.

451
00:28:12,681 --> 00:28:13,801
[SPEAKER_01]: It's coming from the pain.

452
00:28:15,502 --> 00:28:15,742
[SPEAKER_00]: So...

453
00:28:15,762 --> 00:28:17,163
[SPEAKER_00]: The freedom from that pain, yeah.

454
00:28:17,263 --> 00:28:17,463
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

455
00:28:17,603 --> 00:28:18,403
[SPEAKER_01]: Alleviation.

456
00:28:18,483 --> 00:28:19,744
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

457
00:28:19,824 --> 00:28:21,044
[SPEAKER_01]: To whatever extent.

458
00:28:21,925 --> 00:28:29,348
[SPEAKER_01]: So, again, it's important to imagine it on the right basis, which is sensuality, sensual engagement, sensual act.

459
00:28:30,653 --> 00:28:40,420
[SPEAKER_01]: Pleasure of it, that you experience, that you used to experience, that you sometimes still might want to experience, doesn't matter the extent of it.

460
00:28:40,480 --> 00:28:43,962
[SPEAKER_01]: That pleasure is the alleviation of simultaneously enduring pain.

461
00:28:44,563 --> 00:28:47,064
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the pleasure that comes to you through the senses.

462
00:28:50,267 --> 00:28:51,207
[SPEAKER_01]: It never leaves that.

463
00:28:51,688 --> 00:28:54,890
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep assuming pleasure to be external.

464
00:28:54,950 --> 00:28:55,290
[SPEAKER_01]: Hold on.

465
00:28:55,650 --> 00:28:57,852
[SPEAKER_01]: You keep assuming pleasure to be external.

466
00:28:59,087 --> 00:29:01,331
[SPEAKER_01]: to the presently enduring situation that is painful.

467
00:29:01,631 --> 00:29:02,713
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the sensuality.

468
00:29:03,134 --> 00:29:04,015
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is the assumption.

469
00:29:04,677 --> 00:29:04,897
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

470
00:29:05,712 --> 00:29:06,312
[SPEAKER_01]: External?

471
00:29:06,352 --> 00:29:10,773
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you know... how can you know that this is pleasant while you're not engaging with it?

472
00:29:11,033 --> 00:29:13,254
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pleasure... Again, imagine that.

473
00:29:13,574 --> 00:29:14,094
[SPEAKER_01]: Sorry.

474
00:29:14,114 --> 00:29:19,896
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a... there is a prospect of... of sensual, like, touch, of a pleasant nature, but you're not touching it.

475
00:29:20,356 --> 00:29:22,517
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it felt pleasantly, that prospect?

476
00:29:22,557 --> 00:29:26,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you're... if... if the pleasure is in the object of your touch... You're not even touching it.

477
00:29:26,598 --> 00:29:30,459
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you know that it's pleasant now, when you're not touching it?

478
00:29:30,779 --> 00:29:34,780
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, and again, the argument of, oh, because you have a memory from before,

479
00:29:35,300 --> 00:29:36,741
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it still doesn't work.

480
00:29:37,302 --> 00:29:40,324
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you are not elated on account of memory.

481
00:29:40,884 --> 00:29:44,427
[SPEAKER_01]: You're elated on account of the future prospect of, I'm about to touch this.

482
00:29:45,268 --> 00:29:48,270
[SPEAKER_01]: And yet, that's felt pleasantly before you touch it.

483
00:29:48,390 --> 00:29:48,710
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

484
00:29:49,531 --> 00:29:54,535
[SPEAKER_00]: Because it provides the possibility of the alleviation of this pain.

485
00:29:54,655 --> 00:29:54,755
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

486
00:29:55,015 --> 00:29:56,036
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, yeah, okay, yeah.

487
00:29:56,156 --> 00:29:57,217
[SPEAKER_01]: Because the pain is there.

488
00:29:57,817 --> 00:29:58,838
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pain is here.

489
00:30:00,099 --> 00:30:04,002
[SPEAKER_01]: Claiming to get rid of this pain is here, which means prospect that I might be able to is here.

490
00:30:04,909 --> 00:30:10,752
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, whatever pleasure I feel in regard to anything out there, is because of this pain here.

491
00:30:10,772 --> 00:30:12,213
[SPEAKER_01]: And that pain is the pain of sensuality.

492
00:30:12,573 --> 00:30:12,753
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

493
00:30:12,993 --> 00:30:14,934
[SPEAKER_01]: And that pain is the pleasure of sensuality.

494
00:30:15,254 --> 00:30:15,474
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

495
00:30:16,615 --> 00:30:18,336
[SPEAKER_01]: So then, when you go and engage with things...

496
00:30:18,356 --> 00:30:20,217
[SPEAKER_00]: The pleasure of sensuality... ...is within that pain.

497
00:30:20,237 --> 00:30:21,037
[SPEAKER_00]: ...comes from that pain.

498
00:30:21,137 --> 00:30:22,057
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't come from it.

499
00:30:22,738 --> 00:30:23,558
[SPEAKER_01]: It stays within it.

500
00:30:24,058 --> 00:30:24,279
[SPEAKER_00]: Okay.

501
00:30:24,299 --> 00:30:26,119
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the important... That's what I'm trying to... Yeah, yeah, yeah.

502
00:30:26,139 --> 00:30:27,020
[SPEAKER_01]: ...to hammer in here.

503
00:30:28,069 --> 00:30:34,355
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, basically, hammer that in, and there is no basis for assuming external to this experience of sensuality.

504
00:30:34,915 --> 00:30:39,840
[SPEAKER_01]: And you do assume the sense objects to be external to your experience of desire.

505
00:30:40,620 --> 00:30:45,405
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why it's insatiable, because you perverted the order, and this domain of sense object

506
00:30:47,669 --> 00:30:55,656
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, you're not even engaging with it, yet you are feeling pleasure on account of the prospect of engaging with, on the level of the senses.

507
00:30:56,156 --> 00:31:00,440
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a desire there, and that desire is felt like you're delighting in it.

508
00:31:00,940 --> 00:31:01,241
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

509
00:31:01,721 --> 00:31:07,326
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that desire is fully within this pain, that's simultaneously enduring to the same extent.

510
00:31:08,805 --> 00:31:21,716
[SPEAKER_01]: So, if you stop assuming that then the pleasure of alleviating the pain through following that desire is contained external to the pain and the desire, you will stop perverting the order.

511
00:31:23,938 --> 00:31:28,382
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you stop assuming your sense of self to be outside of your experience as a whole.

512
00:31:28,402 --> 00:31:29,763
[SPEAKER_01]: You realize that's inconceivable.

513
00:31:34,293 --> 00:31:35,955
[SPEAKER_00]: Same principle of the same upādāna.

514
00:31:35,975 --> 00:31:36,235
[SPEAKER_00]: Hang on.

515
00:31:36,255 --> 00:31:41,761
[SPEAKER_00]: So, seeing the pleasure as being external to the pain, is basically seeing...

516
00:31:41,781 --> 00:31:42,262
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, there you go.

517
00:31:42,302 --> 00:31:43,043
[SPEAKER_01]: You already said it.

518
00:31:43,764 --> 00:31:46,206
[SPEAKER_01]: A thought of pleasure might be external to the pain.

519
00:31:47,468 --> 00:31:48,148
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you know that?

520
00:31:48,449 --> 00:31:51,192
[SPEAKER_01]: Without engaging with that where the pleasure might be, which you are not.

521
00:31:52,282 --> 00:31:54,245
[SPEAKER_01]: It doesn't matter the extent of your thought.

522
00:31:54,745 --> 00:31:59,732
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said before, like when you say, okay, I think I'm outside of this experience as a whole.

523
00:31:59,772 --> 00:32:01,935
[SPEAKER_01]: Okay, so I'm not outside of this experience as a whole.

524
00:32:02,115 --> 00:32:03,457
[SPEAKER_01]: Something else is.

525
00:32:03,657 --> 00:32:06,461
[SPEAKER_01]: But that can only be the thought that's within this experience as a whole.

526
00:32:07,362 --> 00:32:09,005
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but then there's the outside of that thought.

527
00:32:09,520 --> 00:32:10,761
[SPEAKER_01]: Which can only be within...

528
00:32:11,102 --> 00:32:14,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the same principle in this case, once you start from the right level.

529
00:32:15,326 --> 00:32:22,013
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pleasure that's outside of this desire that has arisen, is within this desire that has arisen.

530
00:32:22,534 --> 00:32:27,439
[SPEAKER_01]: Because how can then you feel it pleasantly, that prospect of that pleasure, unless it's within this desire?

531
00:32:30,122 --> 00:32:32,204
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's pretty much all you need to clarify

532
00:32:32,758 --> 00:32:40,502
[SPEAKER_01]: But all the way, and it will become... well, you will become unable to think that pleasure is in sense objects.

533
00:32:40,882 --> 00:32:44,544
[SPEAKER_01]: The beautiful objects in the world are the ones that have pleasure in them.

534
00:32:45,585 --> 00:32:48,366
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how sensuality falls off.

535
00:32:48,386 --> 00:32:53,989
[SPEAKER_01]: Doesn't mean you... you wouldn't recognize nice food or agreeable touch, but you certainly wouldn't crave for it.

536
00:32:54,669 --> 00:32:54,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

537
00:32:54,929 --> 00:32:57,891
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you don't crave for gratuitous freedom from unpleasantness.

538
00:32:59,627 --> 00:33:03,928
[SPEAKER_00]: Because... You don't crave to alleviate by default of anything that's felt discomfortably.

539
00:33:03,948 --> 00:33:08,150
[SPEAKER_00]: That's because that pleasure cannot possibly undo that more fundamental pain.

540
00:33:08,170 --> 00:33:08,230
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

541
00:33:08,250 --> 00:33:09,510
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always... exactly.

542
00:33:09,630 --> 00:33:10,950
[SPEAKER_00]: It's always within that.

543
00:33:11,231 --> 00:33:11,891
[SPEAKER_00]: So, that's the thing.

544
00:33:11,931 --> 00:33:18,513
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're basically trying to get rid of something by expecting something more particular to get under its roots.

545
00:33:18,633 --> 00:33:19,073
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure.

546
00:33:19,393 --> 00:33:19,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure.

547
00:33:19,993 --> 00:33:25,375
[SPEAKER_00]: Whereas, actually, you... that pain is there whether you like it or not and

548
00:33:28,034 --> 00:33:33,816
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, ironically, you want to be free from it, which is why you engage with centrality.

549
00:33:33,956 --> 00:33:38,598
[SPEAKER_01]: So, all you need to do is recognize that it's painful first, and you want to be free from it.

550
00:33:38,898 --> 00:33:41,899
[SPEAKER_01]: Thus, the best course of action, then, is to not engage with it.

551
00:33:43,260 --> 00:33:53,163
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, that means you now have to be able to endure for as long as it takes, not acting out of the gratuitous assumption of craving in regard to the unpleasant enduring feeling.

552
00:33:55,497 --> 00:34:05,281
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, even when you have a thought, a memory of previous sensuality, a thought of the future sensuality, and it's... and the mind delights in it, you don't deny that.

553
00:34:05,821 --> 00:34:08,402
[SPEAKER_01]: You just see it as included within this pain.

554
00:34:09,242 --> 00:34:19,866
[SPEAKER_01]: And that the pleasure that's felt, even if you were to engage, even when you eat the food that's agreeable, the pleasure you're feeling is the alleviation of the simultaneous enduring pain.

555
00:34:19,886 --> 00:34:20,086
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

556
00:34:20,406 --> 00:34:21,607
[SPEAKER_01]: It's putting pain first.

557
00:34:22,347 --> 00:34:22,507
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

558
00:34:23,188 --> 00:34:25,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Basically... Or not allowing it to be assumed second.

559
00:34:25,770 --> 00:34:26,070
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

560
00:34:26,410 --> 00:34:27,851
[SPEAKER_00]: So, not allowing it to be assumed.

561
00:34:28,012 --> 00:34:32,094
[SPEAKER_01]: So, not... And it gets assumed second when you... when you become careless.

562
00:34:32,615 --> 00:34:34,516
[SPEAKER_00]: When you're basically not mindful of the feeling.

563
00:34:34,536 --> 00:34:34,776
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

564
00:34:34,876 --> 00:34:36,257
[SPEAKER_00]: At the general most... at the general level.

565
00:34:36,758 --> 00:34:38,039
[SPEAKER_02]: Or when you lose sense of strength.

566
00:34:42,722 --> 00:34:45,064
[SPEAKER_02]: So, that... the past or the future...

567
00:34:55,541 --> 00:34:58,104
[SPEAKER_02]: That's pain right here, right now, isn't it?

568
00:34:58,165 --> 00:34:58,585
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

569
00:34:59,086 --> 00:35:00,047
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah.

570
00:35:00,087 --> 00:35:01,429
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another thing, yeah.

571
00:35:01,469 --> 00:35:02,130
[SPEAKER_01]: You can clarify.

572
00:35:02,190 --> 00:35:09,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, how would you know that the past prospects were pleasant, unless you're feeling them now pleasurably?

573
00:35:12,197 --> 00:35:19,843
[SPEAKER_01]: How would you know that the future prospect of engaging with a particular sense object... Oh, why are you feeling it pleasurably right here, right now?

574
00:35:20,704 --> 00:35:30,252
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the extent, doesn't matter the extent, how particular, how committed to a particular aspect of a sense object that you're deriving pleasure from.

575
00:35:30,312 --> 00:35:35,756
[SPEAKER_01]: Any pleasure derived from any sense action is the pleasure of alleviating simultaneously enduring pain.

576
00:35:36,397 --> 00:35:38,539
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not the pleasure that comes from the object.

577
00:35:39,709 --> 00:35:50,619
[SPEAKER_01]: And the reason why you have pressure to engage with the object, is because that's how you give validity to the direction of engagement, which is what alleviates the pain, not gets you the pleasure from the sense object.

578
00:35:50,659 --> 00:35:52,381
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no pleasure in the sense object.

579
00:35:52,441 --> 00:35:57,806
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just objects of perceptions, which are neither pleasurable nor displeasurable.

580
00:35:57,846 --> 00:36:03,391
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, you're feeling pleasurable on account of... and as well, that's why people like to pursue central object.

581
00:36:03,471 --> 00:36:05,013
[SPEAKER_01]: If it's just given, eh...

582
00:36:06,244 --> 00:36:10,347
[SPEAKER_01]: But if it's a bit taken away, taken away, taken away, your pleasure grows.

583
00:36:10,488 --> 00:36:13,090
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would that happen, if the pleasure were truly in the object?

584
00:36:13,110 --> 00:36:14,371
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you haven't touched it yet.

585
00:36:14,531 --> 00:36:16,092
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you know that there's more of it?

586
00:36:16,192 --> 00:36:17,914
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, there is more of it in your mind.

587
00:36:18,975 --> 00:36:33,227
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the desire grows, because the engagement towards that direction grows, because you chose to basically go that way, which means that the... the extent of the prospect of alleviating this also presently enduring pain, grows.

588
00:36:35,235 --> 00:36:42,700
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pressure grows, because you, yourself, made it so.

589
00:36:43,660 --> 00:36:57,729
[SPEAKER_00]: The way the Buddha describes the five strands of sensuality, he says it's like sights that you can be conscious of with the eye, that you can be conscious of, that you could be conscious of.

590
00:36:57,829 --> 00:36:58,810
[SPEAKER_00]: Chakravinyaya.

591
00:37:00,711 --> 00:37:02,292
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's like, it's basically there.

592
00:37:02,312 --> 00:37:03,633
[SPEAKER_00]: He's saying, basically, you are

593
00:37:04,707 --> 00:37:06,568
[SPEAKER_00]: The sensuality is the possibility.

594
00:37:08,309 --> 00:37:08,629
[SPEAKER_00]: Where is it?

595
00:37:08,649 --> 00:37:10,730
[SPEAKER_00]: It's cakravinyaya in the Pali.

596
00:37:10,750 --> 00:37:14,732
[SPEAKER_00]: So, it's sights cognized... a big verb to translate.

597
00:37:14,913 --> 00:37:16,093
[SPEAKER_00]: Sights cognizable, by the way.

598
00:37:16,373 --> 00:37:17,614
[SPEAKER_01]: Cognizable.

599
00:37:17,854 --> 00:37:18,535
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

600
00:37:18,555 --> 00:37:20,656
[SPEAKER_00]: Vinyaya, which means it could be.

601
00:37:20,936 --> 00:37:21,516
[SPEAKER_00]: Sure, sure.

602
00:37:21,536 --> 00:37:22,036
[SPEAKER_00]: Cognized.

603
00:37:22,417 --> 00:37:23,057
[SPEAKER_00]: Not that it is.

604
00:37:23,077 --> 00:37:24,918
[SPEAKER_00]: No, it's all on the level of possibility.

605
00:37:25,058 --> 00:37:25,298
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

606
00:37:26,119 --> 00:37:26,379
[SPEAKER_01]: It is.

607
00:37:26,539 --> 00:37:26,719
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

608
00:37:27,848 --> 00:37:30,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it already starts there, as soon as you're subjected to pain.

609
00:37:30,950 --> 00:37:35,794
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why also the Buddha said, the only way that Putujana knows out of pain, is through sensuality.

610
00:37:37,035 --> 00:37:44,240
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is not out of pain, but it's the only way he knows that they would closely resemble to dealing with pain.

611
00:37:44,300 --> 00:37:46,161
[SPEAKER_01]: It's what he thinks will... Well, no.

612
00:37:46,281 --> 00:37:47,842
[SPEAKER_01]: There is a degree of alleviation.

613
00:37:48,923 --> 00:37:51,005
[SPEAKER_01]: If it weren't so, you wouldn't be pressured by it either.

614
00:37:52,506 --> 00:38:06,269
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's that pursuit, that delight in the possibility of that direction, engaging with it, kind of magically gives the validity of, oh, yes, because I'm doing this, that's why I'm not subjected to this, which you are, obviously, still subjected to.

615
00:38:06,309 --> 00:38:12,051
[SPEAKER_01]: Otherwise, you wouldn't be going down the pursuit, if you're not subjected to the unpleasant desire of it beforehand.

616
00:38:12,351 --> 00:38:13,491
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's the gratification.

617
00:38:13,731 --> 00:38:13,931
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

618
00:38:14,031 --> 00:38:18,872
[SPEAKER_00]: Seeing the gratification is just seeing that any pleasure you could possibly have is still within.

619
00:38:18,912 --> 00:38:21,913
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, as the Buddha said, gratification is on the level of the delight.

620
00:38:22,800 --> 00:38:24,201
[SPEAKER_01]: You're delighting in the prospect.

621
00:38:26,403 --> 00:38:32,869
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you have... when you have, like, something secured, you extend, sort of, like, consuming it.

622
00:38:33,009 --> 00:38:33,970
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure, sure.

623
00:38:34,090 --> 00:38:34,330
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

624
00:38:34,491 --> 00:38:35,111
[SPEAKER_01]: You prolong it.

625
00:38:35,612 --> 00:38:35,792
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

626
00:38:37,253 --> 00:38:42,538
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you just do it, oh, it becomes obvious how it cannot possibly pertain to the domain you're acting from.

627
00:38:54,859 --> 00:39:06,807
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the extent of your sensual cravings is directly determined by the extent of your craving against the painful feeling that you're subjected to.

628
00:39:07,167 --> 00:39:07,788
[SPEAKER_00]: That's all it is.

629
00:39:09,545 --> 00:39:19,231
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any... like when you... and again, as I said, you always start with a... like the... the order you have to start with is the order that's affected with ignorance.

630
00:39:19,811 --> 00:39:21,252
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't start things rightly.

631
00:39:21,832 --> 00:39:24,874
[SPEAKER_01]: So, now, okay, yeah, delighting is... essentiality is a problem.

632
00:39:24,894 --> 00:39:26,314
[SPEAKER_01]: But, oh, I must stop delighting.

633
00:39:26,334 --> 00:39:28,315
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's not quite a method of choice.

634
00:39:28,335 --> 00:39:30,757
[SPEAKER_01]: Delighting is a symptom of an underlying assumption.

635
00:39:31,518 --> 00:39:43,806
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in that case, when you are delighting in the prospect of sensuality, you want to then make sure you include the delight and the prospect of sensual pursuit that you're restraining against, but the prospect is still there.

636
00:39:43,886 --> 00:39:52,192
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you want to include the whole thing, including the future sense object, within the container of pain that's presently enduring.

637
00:39:53,292 --> 00:39:57,195
[SPEAKER_01]: Not left, not right, not before, and certainly not after it.

638
00:39:58,306 --> 00:40:00,987
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that whole assumption that, eh...

639
00:40:01,407 --> 00:40:05,788
[SPEAKER_01]: I have a desire, then I engage and I set, like, satisfy the desire, desire runs away.

640
00:40:06,628 --> 00:40:06,788
[SPEAKER_01]: No.

641
00:40:08,528 --> 00:40:13,449
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just you... you get tired, basically, from running away from the pain, that then you accept it.

642
00:40:14,670 --> 00:40:15,490
[SPEAKER_01]: And it ceases to hurt.

643
00:40:17,030 --> 00:40:18,971
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if you were to accept the pain right away?

644
00:40:19,951 --> 00:40:24,632
[SPEAKER_01]: And removing the middleman of central pursuits that cannot possibly even pertain to the domain of pain.

645
00:40:25,242 --> 00:40:28,605
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you realize you'll be above the pain even before the pain.

646
00:40:32,448 --> 00:40:34,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Which... You can't satisfy your desires.

647
00:40:35,470 --> 00:40:46,299
[SPEAKER_01]: But nobody denies that after a degree of engagement with your sensuality, projected goals of your desires, you don't feel painful anymore.

648
00:40:46,319 --> 00:40:46,839
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

649
00:40:50,022 --> 00:40:52,344
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the sense object remains unchanged.

650
00:40:52,926 --> 00:40:56,149
[SPEAKER_01]: In a way, like you haven't truly consumed it and destroyed it.

651
00:40:56,549 --> 00:40:58,030
[SPEAKER_01]: It's still there, things you've been touching.

652
00:40:58,350 --> 00:41:01,653
[SPEAKER_01]: Even the food, in a way, it's still there, just now in the stomach, as opposed to the table.

653
00:41:02,413 --> 00:41:09,879
[SPEAKER_01]: So, nothing has come out of the material domain, into you, or got consumed by the all-powerful sense of self.

654
00:41:10,880 --> 00:41:11,981
[SPEAKER_01]: Yet, there is no pain now.

655
00:41:12,421 --> 00:41:12,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

656
00:41:12,841 --> 00:41:14,963
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, you just covered it over.

657
00:41:15,023 --> 00:41:15,944
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you cover it over?

658
00:41:16,845 --> 00:41:19,567
[SPEAKER_01]: By... by...

659
00:41:20,510 --> 00:41:24,173
[SPEAKER_01]: How can you cover the door when the domain of senses cannot possibly pertain to the domain of feelings?

660
00:41:26,275 --> 00:41:26,955
[SPEAKER_01]: What has changed?

661
00:41:30,638 --> 00:41:31,959
[SPEAKER_02]: So, you stop perceiving.

662
00:41:33,200 --> 00:41:34,842
[SPEAKER_01]: No, you're not perceiving the pain anyway.

663
00:41:35,102 --> 00:41:35,722
[SPEAKER_01]: You're feeling it.

664
00:41:36,283 --> 00:41:36,943
[SPEAKER_01]: What has changed?

665
00:41:37,123 --> 00:41:37,944
[SPEAKER_01]: Pursuing.

666
00:41:38,765 --> 00:41:41,247
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but you stop pursuing it because it doesn't hurt anymore.

667
00:41:41,727 --> 00:41:42,648
[SPEAKER_01]: The pressure is gone.

668
00:41:42,948 --> 00:41:43,528
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is that?

669
00:41:44,930 --> 00:41:46,411
[SPEAKER_01]: Why is it that it doesn't hurt anymore?

670
00:41:46,431 --> 00:41:47,692
[SPEAKER_01]: Why did it hurt in the first place?

671
00:41:48,793 --> 00:41:50,734
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you were just subjected to a painful feeling.

672
00:41:50,894 --> 00:41:52,235
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's not suffering.

673
00:41:53,015 --> 00:41:54,316
[SPEAKER_01]: Painful feeling is not dukkha.

674
00:41:55,857 --> 00:41:59,019
[SPEAKER_00]: Claiming to be free from painful feeling is what dukkha is.

675
00:41:59,059 --> 00:42:00,600
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's the only thing that changed.

676
00:42:01,240 --> 00:42:09,805
[SPEAKER_01]: After a certain amount of engagement with sensual object, pursuit of it in particular, you stopped resisting the unpleasant feeling in itself.

677
00:42:11,566 --> 00:42:12,587
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you don't suffer.

678
00:42:16,869 --> 00:42:18,470
[SPEAKER_01]: But pursuit of sensual object had nothing

679
00:42:20,831 --> 00:42:32,096
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why, exactly, sometimes, if you keep engaging with the pursuit of the sense-objects, it becomes apparent that you cannot do anything to the pain you're feeling.

680
00:42:32,176 --> 00:42:32,976
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what you do then?

681
00:42:33,056 --> 00:42:33,696
[SPEAKER_01]: You double down.

682
00:42:33,976 --> 00:42:34,637
[SPEAKER_01]: You triple down.

683
00:42:36,137 --> 00:42:39,358
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's how perception of sense-objects proliferates.

684
00:42:39,618 --> 00:42:42,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it has nothing to do with the domain that you're actually acting from.

685
00:42:44,560 --> 00:42:46,181
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, you go to the extremes.

686
00:42:48,480 --> 00:42:56,884
[SPEAKER_01]: Just to... hoping that some of it will allow your mind to take it as a successful project of diversion, basically.

687
00:42:57,745 --> 00:43:00,166
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you don't know how to stop craving the feeling.

688
00:43:00,586 --> 00:43:08,050
[SPEAKER_01]: You're just doing these random things, hoping that it will result in you, at least temporarily, forgetting about resisting the pain.

689
00:43:09,731 --> 00:43:13,413
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why you have to stop engaging with sensuality first.

690
00:43:13,653 --> 00:43:17,435
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if you're completely ignorant, you have to start practicing sense restraint first.

691
00:43:20,570 --> 00:43:27,233
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's basically, it's on the level of a magical belief, a diversion that you know deep down is not... and everybody knows that.

692
00:43:27,954 --> 00:43:30,435
[SPEAKER_01]: I was saying that to Venerable Tanya a while ago.

693
00:43:30,915 --> 00:43:40,080
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, I remember even way before, like, when I was in my teens, before I started reading the Buddhism itself, you always knew deep down you can't satisfy your desires.

694
00:43:40,740 --> 00:43:48,624
[SPEAKER_01]: Talking with friends about it, like, you can't say... yeah, you can't help but pursuing them, but you know already you can't satisfy them.

695
00:43:49,382 --> 00:43:57,229
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the only thing that changes, like, before the pursuit and after pursuit, is your own attitude in regard to what you're already feeling, what you've been feeling all along.

696
00:43:57,849 --> 00:43:58,250
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

697
00:43:59,371 --> 00:44:10,440
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you use sense engagement to tire yourself out, so you can't crave the resistance, you can't maintain the resistance towards unpleasantly enduring feeling, and it ceases to be unpleasant.

698
00:44:12,193 --> 00:44:13,133
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, that's what I was going to say.

699
00:44:13,174 --> 00:44:14,094
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not unpleasant.

700
00:44:14,114 --> 00:44:14,594
[SPEAKER_00]: You don't...

701
00:44:14,994 --> 00:44:15,795
[SPEAKER_00]: It's not unpleasant.

702
00:44:15,855 --> 00:44:16,915
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not suffering, yeah.

703
00:44:17,456 --> 00:44:23,879
[SPEAKER_01]: But habitually, anything disagreeable is resisted, and you make it into suffering.

704
00:44:26,100 --> 00:44:40,187
[SPEAKER_01]: And yeah, many times, and I'm sure everybody experienced that, at some point, even after the culmination of your sensual satisfaction of objects, the mind is still completely unsatisfied, and you want more.

705
00:44:40,487 --> 00:44:41,768
[SPEAKER_01]: The body can't have more.

706
00:44:43,328 --> 00:45:00,698
[SPEAKER_01]: The body's fatigue doesn't... they've each been scratched so much, it hurts, yet, the mental attitude of having their desire completely unchanged, and you're just going mad, basically, with the desire, because you've been giving in to that domain of sense pursuit.

707
00:45:00,798 --> 00:45:03,860
[SPEAKER_01]: They had nothing to do with this, and that's the only way you know.

708
00:45:04,581 --> 00:45:09,904
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, so are you saying that when you've kind of

709
00:45:10,770 --> 00:45:15,976
[SPEAKER_00]: You've gone for, sort of, gratifying your desires and then afterwards you feel, sort of, you feel satisfied.

710
00:45:17,297 --> 00:45:25,246
[SPEAKER_00]: That's because, that's basically because, through your acts, you've actually reduced craving.

711
00:45:27,228 --> 00:45:28,149
[SPEAKER_00]: Because there's no suffering.

712
00:45:31,177 --> 00:45:32,557
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, I won't say you reduced it.

713
00:45:32,577 --> 00:45:32,938
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, exactly.

714
00:45:32,958 --> 00:45:33,858
[SPEAKER_00]: Do you know what I mean?

715
00:45:33,898 --> 00:45:36,100
[SPEAKER_00]: Like, temporarily... Well, you stop doing it.

716
00:45:36,160 --> 00:45:38,041
[SPEAKER_00]: Not the possibility of craving, obviously.

717
00:45:38,061 --> 00:45:38,801
[SPEAKER_01]: Sure, sure, sure.

718
00:45:38,921 --> 00:45:39,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, yeah, no, that's true, yeah.

719
00:45:40,542 --> 00:45:42,223
[SPEAKER_01]: Resistance to suffering is craving.

720
00:45:42,243 --> 00:45:42,383
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

721
00:45:42,724 --> 00:45:44,625
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop resisting when you tire yourself out.

722
00:45:44,805 --> 00:45:48,027
[SPEAKER_00]: So, actually, by scratching, I am actually reducing... You are?

723
00:45:48,067 --> 00:45:48,487
[SPEAKER_01]: You are?

724
00:45:48,567 --> 00:45:50,288
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's why it is gratification.

725
00:45:50,308 --> 00:45:51,069
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're doing it.

726
00:45:51,089 --> 00:45:51,729
[SPEAKER_01]: That's how I started this.

727
00:45:51,749 --> 00:45:52,550
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why you're doing it.

728
00:45:52,570 --> 00:45:55,331
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, if there is no gratification, you wouldn't have called it...

729
00:45:58,681 --> 00:46:04,907
[SPEAKER_01]: And you realize, even the gratification has nothing to do with sense objects, has to do with your own attitude towards your own craving.

730
00:46:05,567 --> 00:46:08,390
[SPEAKER_01]: Towards your own feeling, sorry, which is already an attitude of craving.

731
00:46:09,648 --> 00:46:30,283
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, through scratching your itch, you are reducing craving, but simultaneously making your dependence on running away of pain even harder, even stronger, which means, oh, so through reducing this craving, actually, I am fueling more, like, I'll be more subjected to even more craving later on.

732
00:46:30,843 --> 00:46:34,246
[SPEAKER_03]: Because I need that desire to keep on scratching.

733
00:46:34,266 --> 00:46:35,428
[SPEAKER_03]: Yeah, yeah, exactly.

734
00:46:35,588 --> 00:46:36,869
[SPEAKER_03]: So, it just fuels itself.

735
00:46:36,909 --> 00:46:38,931
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's the only... That's what I mean.

736
00:46:38,971 --> 00:46:50,182
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why first you have to stop doing it, and then you realize that even the acts of doing it were rooted in actually, oh, it's simply because I don't want this pain, as in I gratuitously resist it.

737
00:46:50,482 --> 00:46:52,224
[SPEAKER_01]: But it's not painful if I don't resist it.

738
00:46:52,992 --> 00:46:55,153
[SPEAKER_01]: It's painful if I resist it and then interfere with it.

739
00:46:55,353 --> 00:46:58,334
[SPEAKER_01]: Means, okay, I gave it validity, next time I'll resist it even more.

740
00:46:59,094 --> 00:47:05,537
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what if I... okay, now I stopped engaging with it, stopped maintaining these wrong avenues of trying to deal with it, stopped scratching my itch.

741
00:47:06,431 --> 00:47:10,835
[SPEAKER_01]: What if I now to endure the pain, not act out of it, and then just stop resisting it?

742
00:47:10,895 --> 00:47:11,936
[SPEAKER_01]: Not try to get rid of it.

743
00:47:12,396 --> 00:47:13,057
[SPEAKER_01]: That's another thing.

744
00:47:13,397 --> 00:47:13,517
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

745
00:47:13,537 --> 00:47:14,138
[SPEAKER_00]: Because if people...

746
00:47:14,158 --> 00:47:14,478
[SPEAKER_00]: I must...

747
00:47:14,618 --> 00:47:14,858
[SPEAKER_00]: I must...

748
00:47:14,878 --> 00:47:16,280
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll resist it and then it'll go away.

749
00:47:16,300 --> 00:47:16,820
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

750
00:47:16,940 --> 00:47:17,821
[SPEAKER_01]: Or like a subha.

751
00:47:17,861 --> 00:47:21,704
[SPEAKER_01]: Subha becomes for people getting rid of the the central pain.

752
00:47:21,825 --> 00:47:24,647
[SPEAKER_03]: Because, yeah, I must resist it so it doesn't bother me.

753
00:47:24,807 --> 00:47:25,228
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

754
00:47:25,248 --> 00:47:25,588
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

755
00:47:25,628 --> 00:47:28,510
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the pain is there if it has... if sensuality has arisen.

756
00:47:28,771 --> 00:47:31,253
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sensuality in its nature is harassing.

757
00:47:31,811 --> 00:47:33,632
[SPEAKER_01]: That is the nature of sense objects.

758
00:47:33,732 --> 00:47:34,453
[SPEAKER_01]: They harass you.

759
00:47:34,893 --> 00:47:35,634
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

760
00:47:35,734 --> 00:47:36,835
[SPEAKER_01]: Even before your craving.

761
00:47:36,875 --> 00:47:37,395
[SPEAKER_01]: I see.

762
00:47:37,575 --> 00:47:37,855
[SPEAKER_01]: I see.

763
00:47:37,875 --> 00:47:38,736
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, yeah, yeah.

764
00:47:38,796 --> 00:47:49,944
[SPEAKER_01]: The significance, the implications of a female body for a man, or male body for a woman, or food for a hungry person, the implications are forceful.

765
00:47:51,245 --> 00:47:53,646
[SPEAKER_01]: Even... no effort required.

766
00:47:53,827 --> 00:47:55,568
[SPEAKER_01]: Forceful is felt unpleasantly.

767
00:47:56,674 --> 00:48:03,837
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, automatically, simultaneously, unenlightened mind resists any form of unpleasure.

768
00:48:03,937 --> 00:48:15,482
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you experience forceful perceptions, automatically, you're already resisting them, which means, automatically, you're already delighting in them, to get the freedom from this resistance, that you're responsible for.

769
00:48:15,843 --> 00:48:18,864
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why in Arahant, eh... Aniruddha,

770
00:48:20,512 --> 00:48:26,933
[SPEAKER_01]: When he was... when that woman took him, oh, you can stay in this room, and then came there and took her clothes off, and tried to basically sleep with him.

771
00:48:27,873 --> 00:48:39,135
[SPEAKER_01]: And the sutta says that he just sat cross-legged and was kind of... became mindful of his breathing, and she was sort of dancing, trying to get his attention, then felt embarrassed, because he wasn't... and then dressed up and apologized.

772
00:48:40,176 --> 00:48:43,856
[SPEAKER_01]: I remember the first time I read the sutta, I thought, well, why would Arahant even need to be mindful of his breath?

773
00:48:44,837 --> 00:48:47,117
[SPEAKER_01]: Why would he... like, I thought Arahant would be just completely...

774
00:48:48,882 --> 00:48:49,362
[SPEAKER_01]: Blind.

775
00:48:50,423 --> 00:48:52,225
[SPEAKER_01]: But, no, he was aware of the significance.

776
00:48:52,525 --> 00:48:54,426
[SPEAKER_01]: And that significance is actually harassing.

777
00:48:55,167 --> 00:48:55,827
[SPEAKER_01]: It's unpleasant.

778
00:48:56,548 --> 00:49:00,651
[SPEAKER_01]: So, he was mindful of his breath, just because he was subjected to pain.

779
00:49:01,992 --> 00:49:03,453
[SPEAKER_01]: Not because he tried to get rid of it.

780
00:49:04,634 --> 00:49:11,860
[SPEAKER_01]: So, okay, well, this harassment now, until it stops, I'll just sort of be mindful of my experience as a whole, while I'm being tortured.

781
00:49:12,673 --> 00:49:27,163
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why Rādhāpāla Sutta, when he goes home, already anāgāmī, maybe even an arahant, and the father tells all his wives to dress up provocatively how they used to, how he used to like it, and they go and start trying to seduce him, he says, stop harassing me.

782
00:49:27,183 --> 00:49:27,964
[SPEAKER_01]: This is harassing.

783
00:49:28,064 --> 00:49:30,786
[SPEAKER_01]: Like... And it is.

784
00:49:31,747 --> 00:49:33,047
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's the nature of the senses.

785
00:49:33,147 --> 00:49:39,352
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are harassed by your senses being subjected to these forceful sights, sounds, smells, tastes, touches.

786
00:49:40,533 --> 00:49:41,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, like a dog.

787
00:49:42,054 --> 00:49:42,834
[SPEAKER_01]: He hears something.

788
00:49:42,854 --> 00:49:43,454
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't help it.

789
00:49:43,754 --> 00:49:46,075
[SPEAKER_01]: He can't help it, but he wants to go there now.

790
00:49:46,635 --> 00:49:49,456
[SPEAKER_01]: But you don't need to resist that experience as a whole.

791
00:49:49,656 --> 00:49:54,498
[SPEAKER_00]: That level of harassment, doesn't that apply to any at all?

792
00:49:54,578 --> 00:49:55,238
[SPEAKER_00]: It does.

793
00:49:55,258 --> 00:50:03,401
[SPEAKER_01]: So, basically, whether it's painful or pleasant... That's why, if you were to undo the craving on the absolute fundamental level, sensuality would be undone as well.

794
00:50:03,421 --> 00:50:03,541
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

795
00:50:03,601 --> 00:50:04,722
[SPEAKER_01]: It cannot possibly remain.

796
00:50:05,502 --> 00:50:08,703
[SPEAKER_01]: But, more practically, you might have to address it in itself.

797
00:50:11,594 --> 00:50:12,418
[SPEAKER_02]: Let's go back to that.

798
00:50:16,900 --> 00:50:17,700
[SPEAKER_02]: Yeah.

799
00:50:18,020 --> 00:50:21,281
[SPEAKER_01]: Everything goes... that's the only... that's why I keep saying... the suttas say that.

800
00:50:21,621 --> 00:50:23,181
[SPEAKER_01]: Things converge in feelings.

801
00:50:23,601 --> 00:50:24,021
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

802
00:50:24,521 --> 00:50:26,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Not sometimes, not every other day.

803
00:50:27,042 --> 00:50:27,482
[SPEAKER_01]: Always.

804
00:50:28,202 --> 00:50:31,743
[SPEAKER_01]: Which means your action, your motivation, your perceptions, basically, your moods.

805
00:50:32,663 --> 00:50:33,643
[SPEAKER_01]: Where do they converge?

806
00:50:33,703 --> 00:50:35,663
[SPEAKER_01]: Converge on how you're feeling right here, right now.

807
00:50:35,683 --> 00:50:37,584
[SPEAKER_01]: And that feeling endures.

808
00:50:37,604 --> 00:50:38,244
[SPEAKER_01]: So, here you are.

809
00:50:38,284 --> 00:50:40,124
[SPEAKER_01]: Can't stop thinking about a central object.

810
00:50:40,204 --> 00:50:41,064
[SPEAKER_01]: Where does it converge?

811
00:50:41,404 --> 00:50:44,985
[SPEAKER_01]: Converge on presently enduring feeling of discomfort that you're resisting.

812
00:50:45,674 --> 00:50:50,558
[SPEAKER_01]: You want to get rid of it, but not by any... by means of distracting yourself in this direction.

813
00:50:50,758 --> 00:50:51,118
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

814
00:50:52,239 --> 00:51:02,887
[SPEAKER_01]: And again, imagine... well, imagine being really, really hungry, and you eat something not even so tasty, but it provides you this great pleasure.

815
00:51:03,628 --> 00:51:10,894
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, imagine you're full to the point of feeling sick, and somebody brings something extremely tasty, and you're still sick.

816
00:51:12,615 --> 00:51:13,956
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no pleasure in eating it.

817
00:51:14,790 --> 00:51:20,515
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, if the pleasure were in these sense objects, that wouldn't be the case.

818
00:51:20,635 --> 00:51:23,258
[SPEAKER_01]: Every time you were to eat a pleasant thing, it would be felt pleasantly.

819
00:51:24,179 --> 00:51:26,961
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you're hungry, things that were not pleasant, feel pleasantly.

820
00:51:27,842 --> 00:51:33,527
[SPEAKER_01]: And when you're not hungry, things that are pleasant, feel unpleasantly.

821
00:51:33,547 --> 00:51:34,648
[SPEAKER_01]: So, what's in common to that?

822
00:51:34,708 --> 00:51:38,752
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, it's in common to that whatever your senses are subjected to, is in a way forceful.

823
00:51:41,483 --> 00:51:42,763
[SPEAKER_01]: Agreeable or disagreeable.

824
00:51:42,823 --> 00:51:43,464
[SPEAKER_01]: It pushes you.

825
00:51:43,544 --> 00:51:46,205
[SPEAKER_01]: Significance implies, requires you, pressures you.

826
00:51:46,645 --> 00:51:48,646
[SPEAKER_01]: But, all of that will work.

827
00:51:48,706 --> 00:51:55,128
[SPEAKER_01]: That whole domain of being pressured, implied, pulled, forced upon, will work only if you provide a basis for it.

828
00:51:55,148 --> 00:51:56,988
[SPEAKER_01]: And the basis is, I resist the discomfort.

829
00:51:59,889 --> 00:52:04,231
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's that's how you then become pulled and forced and oppressed.

830
00:52:05,453 --> 00:52:14,763
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop resisting the comfort, none of these things, discomfort, none of these things can possibly enter you, or certainly exercise any influence over you.

831
00:52:16,484 --> 00:52:23,772
[SPEAKER_03]: So the assumption, we have assumption is, there's a prospect of pleasure and

832
00:52:26,236 --> 00:52:31,920
[SPEAKER_03]: I can get that, but you're not recognizing the pain where it's coming from.

833
00:52:31,960 --> 00:52:37,664
[SPEAKER_01]: More precisely, the assumption is, assuming the pleasure to be external to the pain, that's it.

834
00:52:38,925 --> 00:52:39,745
[SPEAKER_01]: External.

835
00:52:40,025 --> 00:52:40,946
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

836
00:52:40,986 --> 00:52:41,687
[SPEAKER_00]: External.

837
00:52:41,767 --> 00:52:48,211
[SPEAKER_01]: So, assuming pleasure to be external to the present enduring pain, that's the assumption of sensuality, isn't it?

838
00:52:50,273 --> 00:52:51,594
[SPEAKER_00]: You can get rid of it or something.

839
00:52:51,614 --> 00:52:52,634
[SPEAKER_00]: Sorry?

840
00:52:52,654 --> 00:52:53,815
[SPEAKER_00]: That's already implied, isn't it?

841
00:52:53,835 --> 00:52:55,536
[SPEAKER_00]: Or it's not connected.

842
00:52:56,256 --> 00:52:56,856
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, that's the thing.

843
00:52:57,217 --> 00:53:02,619
[SPEAKER_01]: Assuming being external to the pain, you already form the external relationship between the two.

844
00:53:02,779 --> 00:53:10,343
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, you can say it's before, it's after, it's not connected, it's fully connected, it's the same, it's different, it's both, it's neither, because you already assumed.

845
00:53:10,363 --> 00:53:17,467
[SPEAKER_00]: The point is, so that it... but basically, it's more that you want that... you assume that that pleasure

846
00:53:18,963 --> 00:53:19,884
[SPEAKER_00]: can get rid of that pain.

847
00:53:20,204 --> 00:53:23,707
[SPEAKER_00]: But that's because, more fundamentally, you see it as external from that pain.

848
00:53:24,227 --> 00:53:26,189
[SPEAKER_00]: And if you see that that pleasure... Yeah.

849
00:53:26,369 --> 00:53:32,133
[SPEAKER_00]: It cannot possibly get rid of that pain, because it... because it isn't separate from that pain.

850
00:53:32,413 --> 00:53:32,814
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within.

851
00:53:32,874 --> 00:53:33,174
[SPEAKER_01]: It's within.

852
00:53:33,194 --> 00:53:33,854
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always within.

853
00:53:33,894 --> 00:53:34,075
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

854
00:53:34,835 --> 00:53:39,619
[SPEAKER_01]: In the same sense, you will stop maintaining the notion of your master controller of the five aggregates.

855
00:53:40,653 --> 00:53:45,997
[SPEAKER_01]: When you realize that any notion you can have about it, of sense of self and master and control, it is within the five aggregates.

856
00:53:46,597 --> 00:53:54,883
[SPEAKER_01]: So, any notion of escape from this presently enduring pain, any notion of pleasure, any thought of, ah, tomorrow I'll experience this, it's within this.

857
00:53:55,143 --> 00:53:58,125
[SPEAKER_01]: So, at that time, to that extent, that is painful.

858
00:53:59,867 --> 00:54:03,269
[SPEAKER_01]: Which is why a prospect of going away from it is pleasant at the same time.

859
00:54:05,646 --> 00:54:12,752
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not like, again, you don't have to wait tomorrow to say no to yourself in regard to sense objects.

860
00:54:12,772 --> 00:54:15,074
[SPEAKER_01]: You can already do it right here, right now, on the level of the mind.

861
00:54:15,114 --> 00:54:16,755
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why sensuality is in the mind.

862
00:54:17,916 --> 00:54:22,240
[SPEAKER_01]: The Buddha gave that symbol, like a man lives completely immersed in sensuality.

863
00:54:22,260 --> 00:54:28,405
[SPEAKER_01]: It's like a man in the river, drenched, soaked, soaking wet, and then he finds two sticks and he wants to light the fire.

864
00:54:28,906 --> 00:54:32,909
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, first he needs to step outside of the river, because he can't light the fire with wet sticks.

865
00:54:33,565 --> 00:54:36,908
[SPEAKER_01]: But then, even when he's up on the shore, he won't be able to light the fire right away.

866
00:54:36,948 --> 00:54:39,950
[SPEAKER_01]: He needs to wait for sticks to dry, for his hands to dry.

867
00:54:40,531 --> 00:54:45,615
[SPEAKER_01]: And he said, so it's like the men who... monks who withdraw from sensuality, but their minds are still wet.

868
00:54:46,756 --> 00:54:50,419
[SPEAKER_01]: But when that dries, then you can... and lighting the fire basically means understanding this.

869
00:54:51,800 --> 00:54:55,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, he can't get wet again, because he's burnt up.

870
00:54:56,883 --> 00:54:57,463
[SPEAKER_01]: But that's the point.

871
00:54:57,483 --> 00:55:08,029
[SPEAKER_01]: So, like, now nothing, you're just kind of generally mindful, you don't have necessarily context of sensual assumptions, and you have a thought of something sensual, that you are about to be subjected to, or could be subjected to.

872
00:55:09,229 --> 00:55:13,211
[SPEAKER_01]: The delight, the extent of that delight, is the extent of your resistance to pain.

873
00:55:13,731 --> 00:55:14,212
[SPEAKER_01]: Nothing else.

874
00:55:15,252 --> 00:55:17,833
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent of that delight is not in the object that you don't have yet.

875
00:55:18,534 --> 00:55:21,435
[SPEAKER_01]: The extent of that delight is the extent of the pain, basically.

876
00:55:22,656 --> 00:55:22,996
[SPEAKER_00]: That's it.

877
00:55:26,947 --> 00:55:31,828
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, yeah, once the skin heals, scratching it, that's unpleasant.

878
00:55:33,288 --> 00:55:38,669
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's the same act of the same centrality, that was felt pleasantly first, because of an underlying condition.

879
00:55:39,129 --> 00:55:45,930
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you put underlying condition aside, an act of scratching is how... how is it felt then?

880
00:55:46,690 --> 00:55:47,551
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always unpleasant.

881
00:55:49,131 --> 00:55:53,552
[SPEAKER_01]: So, experience of sense objects is always unpleasant.

882
00:55:53,892 --> 00:55:55,232
[SPEAKER_01]: You're always harassed by it.

883
00:55:56,636 --> 00:55:57,796
[SPEAKER_00]: And again, that's what Buddha said.

884
00:55:57,816 --> 00:55:58,837
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, exactly.

885
00:55:58,957 --> 00:56:06,080
[SPEAKER_01]: The cow being... whether it leans on the wall, on the tree, sits down, stands, is being consumed by the insect that live in that... on that surface.

886
00:56:07,100 --> 00:56:07,980
[SPEAKER_01]: And that is always.

887
00:56:12,022 --> 00:56:14,663
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's why sensuality, as an attitude, grows.

888
00:56:15,163 --> 00:56:19,425
[SPEAKER_01]: The more you engage with it, the more options you get from it.

889
00:56:22,186 --> 00:56:25,008
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's, again, it always stays with you, never, it can never leave that domain.

890
00:56:25,569 --> 00:56:30,473
[SPEAKER_01]: There is no outside of the sense objects.

891
00:56:30,993 --> 00:56:35,037
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, we spoke about it in regard to the phone, but it's the same principle.

892
00:56:39,300 --> 00:56:41,222
[SPEAKER_00]: If you're touching something, what are you experiencing?

893
00:56:42,392 --> 00:56:45,714
[SPEAKER_01]: The object you're touching, or you're experiencing the experience of your hand touching something.

894
00:56:45,754 --> 00:56:46,335
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly, yeah.

895
00:56:46,435 --> 00:56:46,695
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

896
00:56:46,715 --> 00:57:01,426
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yet, here you are assuming that pleasure is contained in particular, inside of the particular objects that you can't even enter with your own senses, let alone on the level of the mind, somehow, to kind of transcend this domain and enter this domain of physicality.

897
00:57:01,506 --> 00:57:02,506
[SPEAKER_01]: It's inconceivable.

898
00:57:06,609 --> 00:57:08,911
[SPEAKER_03]: And it's inconceivable that it's desire.

899
00:57:11,000 --> 00:57:16,025
[SPEAKER_03]: If I'm maintaining a desire, you're satisfying it.

900
00:57:16,045 --> 00:57:17,286
[SPEAKER_03]: Simultaneous, yeah.

901
00:57:17,746 --> 00:57:18,848
[SPEAKER_03]: Then I'm going to satisfy it.

902
00:57:19,048 --> 00:57:23,292
[SPEAKER_03]: I'm going to keep this thing growing, and it's not going to... it's going to disappear.

903
00:57:23,652 --> 00:57:27,436
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, but it's going to disappear by becoming more and more apparent, yeah.

904
00:57:28,377 --> 00:57:29,018
[SPEAKER_01]: We're getting bigger.

905
00:57:35,312 --> 00:57:39,053
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you do have... again, as I said, everybody starts from a wrong place first.

906
00:57:39,554 --> 00:57:44,936
[SPEAKER_01]: When you do have a thought of sensual object, don't try to negate it or deny it.

907
00:57:45,816 --> 00:57:48,357
[SPEAKER_01]: First, don't act towards it, which would be overly affirming it.

908
00:57:49,437 --> 00:57:51,078
[SPEAKER_01]: But now, don't try to deny it either.

909
00:57:51,538 --> 00:57:52,358
[SPEAKER_01]: Allow it to endure.

910
00:57:52,418 --> 00:57:52,718
[SPEAKER_01]: Why?

911
00:57:52,738 --> 00:57:56,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Because that's how you can discern a context greater than the thought of sensuality.

912
00:57:59,210 --> 00:58:00,391
[SPEAKER_01]: And this is the context created.

913
00:58:00,411 --> 00:58:01,091
[SPEAKER_01]: We just described it.

914
00:58:01,111 --> 00:58:02,532
[SPEAKER_01]: So, oh, I... there is again...

915
00:58:03,232 --> 00:58:03,592
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't know.

916
00:58:03,852 --> 00:58:06,133
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm hungry and there is a nice food on the table.

917
00:58:07,374 --> 00:58:09,275
[SPEAKER_01]: And there is a delightful prospect.

918
00:58:09,355 --> 00:58:10,296
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm delighting in it.

919
00:58:11,416 --> 00:58:12,477
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm not acting out of it.

920
00:58:13,097 --> 00:58:16,219
[SPEAKER_01]: So, I'm practicing restraint, but the delight is there.

921
00:58:16,419 --> 00:58:18,440
[SPEAKER_01]: But if I'm like, oh, I shouldn't feel this, I shouldn't feel it.

922
00:58:18,520 --> 00:58:19,300
[SPEAKER_01]: Oh, now I still...

923
00:58:19,320 --> 00:58:20,241
[SPEAKER_01]: I'm trying to get rid of it.

924
00:58:20,841 --> 00:58:22,101
[SPEAKER_01]: First, I try to go for it.

925
00:58:22,242 --> 00:58:23,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Now, I try to go against it.

926
00:58:23,442 --> 00:58:24,923
[SPEAKER_01]: Either way, I want to get rid of it.

927
00:58:25,957 --> 00:58:27,579
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you must stop trying to get rid of it.

928
00:58:28,199 --> 00:58:35,506
[SPEAKER_01]: So, once you stop... once you start restraining and don't, as I said, overly affirming to your physical action, you do have to learn how to allow it to endure.

929
00:58:35,886 --> 00:58:36,527
[SPEAKER_01]: Not mentally.

930
00:58:36,547 --> 00:58:37,007
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want it.

931
00:58:37,067 --> 00:58:37,407
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want it.

932
00:58:37,487 --> 00:58:37,888
[SPEAKER_01]: I don't want it.

933
00:58:38,348 --> 00:58:39,990
[SPEAKER_01]: Because you didn't create it to begin with.

934
00:58:40,070 --> 00:58:40,891
[SPEAKER_01]: You're subjected to it.

935
00:58:41,191 --> 00:58:43,753
[SPEAKER_01]: And your only freedom from what you're subjected to is to fully understand it.

936
00:58:43,813 --> 00:58:45,675
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't understand it if you keep denying it.

937
00:58:47,115 --> 00:58:52,777
[SPEAKER_01]: So, food is on the table, I'm very hungry, but I'm restraining myself, it hasn't been offered, I'm not gonna eat it.

938
00:58:52,837 --> 00:58:57,339
[SPEAKER_01]: Now I have the delight in my mind, the prospect of eating it, the prospect of somebody coming and offering me, and I'm eating it, and so on.

939
00:58:59,659 --> 00:59:00,720
[SPEAKER_01]: How do you free yourself from that?

940
00:59:02,400 --> 00:59:07,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Or how do you regain perspective in regard to that directional prospect?

941
00:59:11,080 --> 00:59:13,221
[SPEAKER_01]: As I said, would you be eating the food?

942
00:59:13,481 --> 00:59:13,681
[SPEAKER_00]: No.

943
00:59:13,861 --> 00:59:20,544
[SPEAKER_01]: First of all... Or would you be experiencing your mouth being subjected to food?

944
00:59:21,044 --> 00:59:23,806
[SPEAKER_01]: See, that's the context that you can easily ignore.

945
00:59:25,406 --> 00:59:38,272
[SPEAKER_01]: But you realize, if you make an effort to not ignore it, you would actually shift the emphasis from the food, food, food, food, to, oh, these random senses that I was given, subjected to, are then subjected to further things.

946
00:59:39,423 --> 00:59:49,389
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if those things are agreeable or disagreeable, you stop fearing them, because now you're at the right basis of not being careless in regard to the... As I said, can you experience the food?

947
00:59:49,669 --> 00:59:53,171
[SPEAKER_01]: Can you experience any sense object if your senses are not there operating, living?

948
00:59:53,391 --> 00:59:55,292
[SPEAKER_01]: If there is no sixth sense base present?

949
00:59:56,193 --> 00:59:58,074
[SPEAKER_01]: If the body is not there functional?

950
00:59:58,114 --> 00:59:58,995
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't.

951
00:59:59,055 --> 00:59:59,575
[SPEAKER_01]: Impossible.

952
01:00:00,095 --> 01:00:02,377
[SPEAKER_01]: So you realize, it doesn't matter how much I forget this,

953
01:00:03,413 --> 01:00:04,514
[SPEAKER_01]: It is the right order of things.

954
01:00:04,654 --> 01:00:05,495
[SPEAKER_01]: The body comes first.

955
01:00:05,515 --> 01:00:08,397
[SPEAKER_01]: The necessity of the six senses comes first.

956
01:00:09,017 --> 01:00:21,706
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah, they're only known through engagement with sense objects, but, again, I cannot abandon them to go to sense objects, because my only formal relationship is within them that relate to sense objects.

957
01:00:21,726 --> 01:00:24,588
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you stop assuming external to your six senses.

958
01:00:24,628 --> 01:00:25,789
[SPEAKER_01]: Again, upādāna andhāna.

959
01:00:26,249 --> 01:00:28,771
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop assuming external to the sense of five aggregates.

960
01:00:28,951 --> 01:00:29,872
[SPEAKER_01]: ātavādā andhāna.

961
01:00:30,775 --> 01:00:34,260
[SPEAKER_01]: You stop assuming external to the basically presently enduring feeling.

962
01:00:35,342 --> 01:00:40,690
[SPEAKER_01]: Means you stop craving against the presently enduring feeling, whether against the words or against the way from it.

963
01:00:41,210 --> 01:00:42,092
[SPEAKER_01]: Upādāna, I'm done.

964
01:00:43,974 --> 01:00:44,956
[SPEAKER_01]: It's always the same principle.

965
01:00:44,976 --> 01:00:46,158
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why it's always upādāna.

